{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/pulls/46811","id":3941089400,"node_id":"PR_kwDOI7xefs7q6EB4","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46811","diff_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46811.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46811.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/46811","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/pulls/46811/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/pulls/46811/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/46811/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/statuses/f69793c76ebf1a8f02bb8252c0db868ecd98c254","number":46811,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"[Frontend] Add low-overhead /ready GPU health check","user":{"login":"anencore94","id":37469330,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM3NDY5MzMw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/37469330?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94","html_url":"https://github.com/anencore94","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anencore94/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Purpose\n\nFix #36960\n\nAdd a `/ready` readiness endpoint that checks whether the engine can serve requests. The existing `/health` endpoint stays as the cheap liveness check.\n\n`/ready` calls `EngineClient.check_health_gpu()`. In `AsyncLLM` it does three things:\n\n1. Runs the existing `check_health()` liveness check. A dead engine returns 503.\n2. Sends one EngineCore utility call, `check_ready()`, which runs on the engine-core thread between steps:\n    - If the engine is sleeping or paused (`EngineCore.is_sleeping()`), it returns not ready and runs nothing.\n    - If no request from any API server is in flight (`scheduler.has_requests()` is false and `batch_queue` is empty), it runs `execute_dummy_batch()` to confirm a forward pass still works.\n    - Otherwise it returns ready without adding GPU work.\n3. Waits at most `VLLM_HEALTH_CHECK_GPU_TIMEOUT` seconds (default 20). A timeout or an exception returns 503.\n\nThis borrows the low-overhead idea from SGLang's health generation path. Instead of routing a synthetic request through the scheduler, it uses vLLM's existing utility-call dummy batch.\n\n### Behaviour details\n\n- **MoE with DP (`DPEngineCoreProc`)**: `check_ready()` is overridden and never runs the dummy batch. A batch started outside the DP wave would enter DP collectives without its peer ranks. On these engines `/ready` reports liveness and sleep/pause state only.\n- **Dense with DP**: each rank is an independent `EngineCoreProc` (`reconfigure_for_independent_dp_rank()`), so the probe runs.\n    - With an external LB, each API server probes its own rank.\n    - With the internal LB, `DPLBAsyncMPClient` sends the probe to every rank it manages. An exception or hang on any rank gives 503. The value returned is the first rank's, and sleep/pause are broadcast to all ranks the same way.\n- **Busy engine**: no GPU work is added, but the check is still a round trip through the engine-core loop.\n    - If that loop is blocked inside a step, the call is not answered and `/ready` returns 503 after the timeout.\n    - On a healthy busy engine the call waits for the current step to finish, so a readiness probe `timeoutSeconds` should exceed the longest expected step (for example a long prefill).\n    - A loop that keeps running without completing model steps is not detected. That needs forward-progress tracking (see #59179, #43122, #45526).\n- **Single-flight**: each `AsyncLLM` has at most one probe in flight. Concurrent callers, and callers that arrive after an earlier caller timed out, await the same shielded task. A wedged engine therefore has at most one queued `check_ready` per API server. A caller that times out gets 503. A caller that is cancelled re-raises the cancellation and does not cancel the shared probe.\n- **Multiple API servers**: the idle check runs inside EngineCore on the same thread as the dummy batch. It sees requests from every API server, and no request can arrive between the check and the batch.\n- **Sleep/pause**: returns 503. Behind a readiness-gated Kubernetes Service, a sleeping or paused pod leaves the endpoints, so wake-up and resume must be sent to the pod address directly. This is documented.\n- **`EngineClient.check_health_gpu`** is a plain method that raises `NotImplementedError`, following `scale_elastic_ep` and `collective_rpc`. It is not abstract. For out-of-tree clients that do not implement it, `/ready` falls back to the `/health` answer.\n- **Rust frontend**: `/ready` is served only by the Python frontend. The e2e test is skipped under `VLLM_USE_RUST_FRONTEND=1`.\n- Like `/health`, `/ready` is unauthenticated and excluded from Prometheus HTTP metrics.\n\n## Why this is not duplicating existing PRs\n\nDuplicate-work checks:\n\n```bash\ngh issue view 36960 --repo vllm-project/vllm --comments\ngh pr list --repo vllm-project/vllm --state open --search \"36960 in:body\"\ngh pr list --repo vllm-project/vllm --state open --search \"health endpoint\"\ngh pr list --repo vllm-project/vllm --state open --search \"readiness\"\n```\n\n- #43122 (readiness plus EngineCore busy-period progress tracking) and #45526 (`/health/decode`, decode forward progress) detect stalls while requests are running. With no traffic, they report idle.\n- #36451 (alive-but-hung EngineCore detection) proves that EngineCore answers over IPC. It does not run a forward pass.\n- #45551 (`/readiness/stages`) reports startup stages and the expected delay until the model is ready, for autoscaling. It does not probe execution.\n- #59179 (@lio1226, opened 2026-09-29) adds the same `/ready` endpoint, busy-engine forward-progress tracking and DP supervisor aggregation. It follows a combined design @lio1226 posted in this thread on 2026-09-15. Its idle-probe part overlaps with this PR, which now includes @matteso1's review fixes. The split I proposed in this thread: this PR lands the endpoint and the idle probe, and the busy-progress and supervisor work follows on top of it.\n- #36961 was my earlier draft for this issue. I closed it in favour of this branch.\n\n## Failure-mode positioning\n\n- #36451 (IPC ping): EngineCore answers over the utility/ZMQ path. No forward pass is run.\n- #43122, #45526, #59179 (progress tracking): catch a busy engine that stops completing steps. They report idle when there is no traffic.\n- This PR: runs a forward pass when the engine is idle, so it catches a faulty GPU or executor that nothing else is exercising. When the engine is busy, it only confirms that the core loop answers within the timeout.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add `GET /ready`. It is excluded from Prometheus HTTP metrics and listed in `docs/serving/online_serving/README.md` and `docs/usage/security.md`.\n- Add `EngineClient.check_health_gpu()`. It is not abstract and raises `NotImplementedError`.\n- Add the `EngineCore.check_ready()` utility, and a `DPEngineCoreProc.check_ready()` override that never runs the dummy batch.\n- Add `AsyncMPClient.check_ready_async()`.\n- Implement `AsyncLLM.check_health_gpu()` with a single-flight, shielded probe.\n- Add the `VLLM_HEALTH_CHECK_GPU_TIMEOUT` env var. The default is 20, and a non-positive value falls back to 20. It is in the `compile_factors` ignore list.\n- Tests:\n    - Unit tests for `AsyncLLM`, `EngineCore` and `DPEngineCoreProc`, and for the HTTP handler.\n    - An e2e `/ready` test, skipped under the Rust frontend.\n    - The forward-error shutdown test now asserts that `check_health_gpu()` raises `EngineDeadError`.\n\n## Test Plan / Results\n\nLocal runs, macOS arm64, CPU only:\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/v1/engine/test_async_llm_health.py -q\n# 13 passed\n\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/entrypoints/serve/instrumentator/test_basic.py -q \\\n  -k \"health_ready or ready_route or health_check_engine_dead_error\"\n# 7 passed, 6 deselected\n\nVLLM_USE_RUST_FRONTEND=1 .venv/bin/python -m pytest \\\n  tests/entrypoints/serve/instrumentator/test_basic.py::test_check_ready -q -rs\n# 1 skipped: /ready is not implemented in the Rust frontend\n\npre-commit run --files $(git diff --name-only origin/main...HEAD)\n# all applicable hooks passed: ruff check, ruff format, typos, markdownlint,\n# mypy (3.10), SPDX, test tethering, forbidden imports, config validation, ...\npre-commit run mypy-3.12 --hook-stage manual --files <changed .py files>\n# passed\n```\n\nOn this machine every test also reports a conftest teardown error: `device_allocator INTERNAL ASSERT FAILED ... Allocator for mps is not a DeviceAllocator`. All 20 errors are at teardown. There are no setup errors and no test-body failures. The error comes from the local MPS environment.\n\nThe unit tests cover:\n\n- Readiness is reported as the engine returns it: ready, or not ready while sleeping or paused.\n- A dead engine raises `EngineDeadError` before any utility call.\n- A failed dummy batch reports not ready.\n- Single-flight: 4 concurrent callers result in one utility call.\n- After a timeout, no second probe is enqueued while the first is pending, and the slot is cleared once the probe finishes.\n- The exception of an abandoned probe is still retrieved.\n- `EngineCore.check_ready()` in the idle, requests-pending, batch-in-flight and sleeping cases.\n- `DPEngineCoreProc.check_ready()` never runs the dummy batch.\n- The HTTP handler returns 200 or 503, and falls back to liveness for clients without `check_health_gpu`.\n\nMutation checks:\n\n- Making `DPEngineCoreProc.check_ready()` call `super()` fails `test_dp_engine_core_check_ready_skips_dummy_batch[False]`.\n- Removing the single-flight guard fails `test_check_health_gpu_concurrent_callers_share_one_probe` and `test_check_health_gpu_timeout_does_not_enqueue_second_probe`.\n\nThese need CI or a GPU and cannot run locally:\n\n- `test_basic.py::test_check_ready`, the e2e test against a real server.\n- `tests/v1/shutdown/test_forward_error.py`.\n\nModel evaluation: not applicable. This adds a health endpoint. Model outputs and the request path are unchanged, and the dummy batch runs only when no request is in flight.\n\n## CI status\n\nRebased onto current `main` (741edeebee). New commits do not start upstream CI, so this head needs a maintainer or trusted contributor to comment `/ci run`.\n\n## AI Assistance\n\nAI assistance (Claude Code) was used for the implementation, the tests, this description and the review replies. I reviewed every changed line and ran the tests listed above.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","labels":[{"id":5135047180,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABMhKaDA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/documentation","name":"documentation","description":"Improvements or additions to documentation","color":"0075ca","default":true,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":6579171167,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABiCYvXw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/frontend","name":"frontend","description":"","color":"80D2AC","default":false,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":7614859318,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAA